1079 #uuid "0759db57-b8ce-5f50-b46e-8b442e52ee62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186536484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536741 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5d7987cd-9189-59ea-8be9-6b86123a7745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536784 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536784 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536784 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186536652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536784 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c43acc61-3f4b-58f8-9442-6e3ef8f460f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536833 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{12n+1}{3n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "548006ed-ff60-5fe8-9b17-af5147ac7aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536833 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}{\\frac{3n}{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12+\\frac{1}{n}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12+0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536882 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d26b4fec-f259-5757-943d-c92ed32d5a4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536882 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $10{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{10{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(10-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $\\frac{2}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536898 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f593ac8e-7a29-5f43-82ff-aea84a5ba69c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536898 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}=\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-9{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{n}-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-0}{0-9} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06677fb3-7ea6-5165-8cd4-81f9a96fa023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536904 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07528adf-f296-5070-8943-009a2dae445f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1718a7b4-2d06-538f-8e12-c06876a48a52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186416142 #edb/ref 1759218604541